hi there steve kaufman here today to  talk about language learning again of  course  and to talk specifically about managing  your time time management  now  if you enjoy these videos please  subscribe and if you like the video give  me a thumbs up if you want  um first of all why do i even do these  videos you know or why do i spend so  much time on language learning  it's because i like doing it i enjoy  learning languages it gives me a lot it  fills my life with  things that are interesting to me  i could be doing other things who knows  why we  devote ourselves to  this or that activity  it's important though to think that what  we are doing is worthwhile then we  derive satisfaction from doing it and  that's certainly my approach to language  learning i enjoy doing it i enjoy  sharing my enthusiasm with others  and sharing my experience with others i  probably spend more time on language  learning than most people  so to that extent what i do may be a  valuable  point of reference for others  so  managing our time so i'm going to talk  about three points actually  obviously right now where i'm committing  myself  to learning three languages  every day  arabic persian and turkish  time management becomes an issue  otherwise i'll just do one of these  languages and run out of time to do the  others  or won't find enough time to put any  significant amount of time in any of  them  so the first thing i've done is to make  sure that i have some very specific  concrete goals not a general goal like i  want to speak three languages  but rather specific goals the number one  goal i've set for myself is to create a  hundred links every day in each language  that means  creating links people ask what does that  mean if you go to  my website the one that i use that i've  co-founded with my son  lingq.com  you will see that the act of looking up  a word that we have not seen before  which is highlighted in blue on the  system that that is called creating a  link you then see  a number of possible meanings for that  word it's then saved to your database  and a number of  functions and and assists to learning  that word and learning the language are  activated on the website i did that the  other day i uh dropped my remote control  for the uh shouldn't leave it in my lap  so  concrete goals goal number one for me is  create a hundred links in each language  that means that once i reach 100 i go to  the next language so once i've gone  through all three languages at least  i've covered that so that's a concrete  goal  the other goals are the goals that i  think drive language learning for me are  creating links  which me that means you're forcing  yourself into new material you're  discovering new material you're not just  staying with things that are easy and  familiar because to find blue words  words you haven't seen before you have  to move forward into  material you haven't read before  the second thing is the overall number  of words you read is an important  counter so i do watch it  the the sort of linking activity  creating these links drives me but i  want to make sure that  i read  material where i've perhaps already gone  through and created the links but i'll  read them again  and the third major driver of my  language learning is listening but the  listening is is much easier because i  listen  whenever i can i don't deliberately sit  down to listen so i listen while doing  the dishes i listen in the car i listen  while exercising i listen on the bicycle  and  i listen now of course since i'm now  doing three languages if i look at my  statistics  i find that  my reading number if i look over the  last month let's say  looking at my statistics in link and you  can go to your profile and you see the  sort of  you know last week last two weeks last  month last three months whatever you  want to look at you'll see what you've  what your level of activity has been  and i see that my number of links is off  the charts because nobody is creating  300 links a day like i am so that i'm  well beyond the sort of targets that  link assigns  on my reading i'm more or less keeping  up to my target in each language that  means i'm reading a lot combined but of  course in each  spec you know individual language i'm  more or less keeping up to  the uh  the targets that link so about 15 000  words in each of those languages when it  comes to the listening i'm doing less  listening than is recommended because  i'm dividing my listening up between the  three languages and the listening is  usually just  the dead time when i have when i'm doing  something else and therefore i listen  so i have those goals the driving goal  is to create links but i do follow my  reading number and my listening number  the rest will take care of them  themselves like converting links to  known  known words all of that will naturally  increase as i continue listening linking  listening and reading  because as i come across words that i've  linked before that i see for the third  fourth fifth eighth time eventually i  moved them to no  so those are the goals so that's point  number one in terms of time management  point number two  be prepared to experiment find  you know which way of learning which way  of using your time works best for you  for example when i started i would just  think of making sure that i save 100  links every day and then i'd find that  it would be 10 30 at night and i'd look  up my statistics and i'll only add 50  links in turkish or in arabic so then i  have to sit there at 10 30 at night and  go through material which i don't really  like to do because i like to before  going to bed i'll read a book in english  on something  i don't necessarily want to be under the  gun to create 100 links at 10 30 at  night  so what i've now decided to do is the  first thing in the morning i create my  hundred links in each language i have  also decided that i am going to go  through wherever i find material that  has a lot of  unknown words  i go through and i just link the blue  words i don't read the words in between  because it takes too long especially in  arabic and persian where i'm reading in  the arabic script which  i'm not very good at so it's very goes  very slowly turkish is a lot faster  because it's written in the latin  alphabet so i go through there  before my wife gets up i've gone through  and i've knocked off a hundred links  created in each of three languages now i  can relax so i've got that behind me  now that's something that i just started  doing a week ago we'll see how that goes  but so far i'm enjoying it i think  that's a good way to go  then  after that i find time to read  and when i read typically i'm going to  find yellow words previously saved links  where i now know those words  but i'm not setting myself a target  i'm just when i come across them  i move them to known or i move them from  status 1 to status 2 or status three  and and by the same token when i  complete re-reading  a lesson i make sure  uh you know if this is the you know  again it's explaining how link works but  if i go through a lesson just linking  then  the system thinks i've read that lesson  so if i read it a second time  i don't manually add  reading  however if i read it a third time i  might manually add the reading number  although the manually added reading  number is not counted in your activity  index  something to think of so in a way i'm  cheating because when i go through  they're creating those links the system  thinks i've read that article or that  lesson but i haven't but then i make a  point of going back  to read again  listen and read  to make sure i go over that material  properly and read it because reading is  very important  so i experiment now it may be in two  weeks that i find that there's some  other combination of these activities  that works better for me but right now  i'm enjoying it because i find that even  going through and linking a bunch of new  words it is exposure to these words at  some level and then when i go back in  later on i pick up on i've kind of gone  over the same material a second time  this time through going through every  word whereas before i was just jumping  word to word and of course a lot of this  material is for example podcasts that  i've downloaded so then i listen to  these so i'm going over  sort of the same material  in different ways  understanding parts of it each time  but i do sense definitely that i hear  the three languages more clearly i am  making more sense of these three  languages so so far i feel this is  working and that brings me to the third  element of managing your time  so first of all set yourself goals  be don't be  afraid to experiment and to vary your  activities but third of all  whatever you're doing  try to enjoy it and try to believe that  what you are doing is worthwhile i  believe that what i am now doing is  worthwhile that it is working that i am  improving that i understand these  podcasts when i listen to them much  better than i did before that's my  impression i feel good about what i'm  doing i don't wish i were doing  something else i don't wish that i were  i don't know  reviewing words in a anki deck or  whatever however if you like reviewing  words in an anki deck  make sure you enjoy doing that and that  you believe that that is worthwhile  doing so you know managing your time a  part of that is also  to feel that whatever activity you're  committed to doing or whatever method of  learning you use  you think that's a good use of your time  you feel happy about what you're doing i  think that's an important an important  element of managing your time sort of  staying in the moment and thinking that  this is where i want to be and this is  what i want to be doing so there you  have it that's my little  spiel with reference to my own  experience on managing your time as a  language learner again if you would like  to receive my 10 emails on 10 tips on  language learning please click on the  button here on the screen  and i look forward to your comments bye  for now
 

